The Phantom of Oranje

♫2360978189

The canals of the Amstel were ripples of gray,  
When a skinny street-kid turned the grass into play.  
With a shift of his hips and a twitch of his boot,  
He tore up the pitch from the branch to the root.  
They called him Johan, the white-shirt fourteen,  
The sharpest conductor the world’s ever seen.

With Ajax he built a magnificent throne,  
A brand of the play that was uniquely their own.  
“Total Football” arose like a wave from the sea—  
Where defenders could attack and the wingers flew free.  
Three European Cups they held up to the sky,  
As the beautiful game learned to gallop and fly.

× × ×

Then came seventy-four, and the ultimate test,  
The Oranje brigade was the world’s favorite guest.  
They coasted to Munchen with effortless grace,  
Smoked cigarettes, laughed, and set a fierce pace.  
In the final’s first minute, before the West German host  
Had even touched leather, Cruyff struck at their post.

He tore through the backline, drew blood and a foul,  
And Neeskens fired home to a thunderous howl.  
But the Germans were iron, stubborn and deep,  
While the Dutch grew complacent and fell fast asleep.  
Müller scored low, and the whistle was blown—  
The greatest of teams lost the ultimate throne.

Cruyff took the defeat with his visor held high,  
“They won the gold medal, but we won the sky.”  
He refused Argentina four years down the line,  
Defying the generals, their threats, and design.  
He walked from the Cup just to stay with his bride,  
Choosing his freedom o’er national pride.

× × ×

He traveled to Spain where the Catalans bled,  
And gave back their pride in the blue and the red.  
As a player he conquered, but then as the boss,  
He rebuilt Barcelona from failure and loss.  
He gathered his “Dream Team,” brave, passing, and smart,  
And turned the Camp No  
        to a palace of art.

He smoked through his bypass, he spoke with a sneer,  
Confronting directors with no sign of fear.  
“If you have the ball, then the other side can’t score,”  
Was the arrogant gospel he brought to the floor.  
They sacked him at last in a boardroom of spite,  
But he’d already left them a permanent light.

× × ×

A phantom of orange, a flash in the box,  
The thinker who broke every tactical locks.  
He died in the spring when his lungs gave out breath,  
But his beautiful system outwitted his death.  
The stadium echoes, the legacy burns:  
The world keeps on spinning where   
Johan  
        Cruyff  
                turns.
